===Motivation===

A descriptor's <code>KEY</code> expression can be a hex public key, a WIF private key, or a base58 xpub/xprv, any of
which may need to survive being copied by hand, printed, or transmitted through a channel (QR, voice, a paper card)
prone to transcription errors. None of these formats has BIP-0093's checksum, error-detection/-correction, or
Shamir-secret-sharing properties: a single mistyped character in an xpub either fails opaquely or, worse, silently
resolves to a different valid xpub. Extending codex32 to this material gives it the same guarantees a codex32 seed
backup already has.

==Specification (sketch)==

===HRP registry===

{| class="wikitable"
! HRP !! Meaning !! Payload
|-
| <code>pk</code> || Public key || 33 bytes (compressed) or 65 bytes (uncompressed)
|-
| <code>xo</code> || X-only public key (BIP-0340) || 32 bytes
|-
| <code>wif</code> / <code>twif</code> || Private key, mainnet / testnet || 32-byte scalar plus a compression-status character (see Open questions)
|-
| <code>xpub</code> / <code>xprv</code> / <code>tpub</code> / <code>tprv</code> || BIP-0032 extended key, mainnet/testnet public/private || depth, parent fingerprint, child number, chain code, key (74 bytes; the codex32 HRP replaces the base58 version bytes)
|-
| ''undecided'' (<code>mn</code> or <code>rn</code>) || Combined chain code + private key ("root node"), for a BIP-0032 master key with no depth/fingerprint/child-number fields || 64 bytes
|}

<code>pk</code> and <code>xo</code> deliberately have no network-tagged variant, matching BIP-0380's own hex pubkeys:
the key material is network-agnostic, and network is a property of the address/script built from it, not the key.

An xpub or xprv's own fingerprint (first 32 bits of Hash160 of its pubkey) MAY be used as its codex32 identifier by
default only for HRPs whose fingerprint carries no separate correlation risk (<code>xo</code>). For <code>pk</code>,
the fingerprint doubles as the P2PKH/P2WPKH address's own hash160, so using it as a share identifier would let a
single below-threshold share leak which on-chain address(es) it belongs to; other HRPs should not default to it
either without the same analysis.

===Key-origin information===

Open question: encode <code>[fingerprint/path]</code> exactly as BIP-0380 does, as a bracketed prefix outside the
codex32 string, or offer folding it into the codex32 HRP itself? See Open questions.

==Rationale==

'''Why <code>pk</code> and not <code>pub</code>?''' Consistency with the descriptor function names that already mean
"public key" everywhere in BIP-0380/381/382 (<code>pk()</code>, <code>pkh()</code>, <code>wpkh()</code>), and it's
shorter. It reads like "private key" out of context, but not inside a codex32/descriptor context where every use of
"pk" already means public key.

'''Why not shorten <code>wif</code>/<code>twif</code> further?''' "WIF" is already a maximally-compressed,
universally-recognized term with no equally-recognized shorter form to fall back on, unlike <code>pub</code>→<code>pk</code>
which had one ready to reuse. <code>twf</code>, <code>twi</code>, and <code>wit</code> (the last easily confused with
"witness") are all less recognizable than keeping the existing name with a <code>t</code> prefix, matching
<code>tpub</code>/<code>tprv</code>.

'''Why does <code>pk</code>/<code>xo</code>/<code>xpub</code>/<code>tpub</code> generation require padding rather
than free choice, unlike a seed?''' These payloads embed an actual elliptic-curve public key, which must satisfy
curve membership; a randomly chosen payload essentially never does. BIP-0093's "fresh secret" procedure (freely
choose payload symbols) therefore doesn't apply to these HRPs the way it does to a seed — implementations MUST treat
the last symbol(s) as fixed padding rather than free choice for these types. This does not apply to
<code>xprv</code>/<code>tprv</code>/<code>wif</code>/<code>twif</code>: a private scalar has no membership
constraint, so free choice remains valid there.

==Backwards compatibility==

New HRPs under the generalized-HRP codex32 grammar; no effect on existing "ms"/"cl"-HRP codex32 strings or on
BIP-0380 descriptors that don't use codex32-encoded keys.

==Open questions==

* Key-origin info: bracketed BIP-0380 syntax outside the codex32 string, or an HRP-folded form? Folding it in is a
  real QR-size reduction for multisig descriptors (origin info is often the largest contributor to a cosigner-heavy
  descriptor's QR footprint), at the cost of a second way to write the same information.
* WIF compression status: a dedicated first character (two reserved charset values, e.g. <code>q</code>=compressed /
  <code>p</code>=uncompressed) versus folding the flag into the payload's existing padding bits. The padding-bit form
  is one character shorter but exactly as unreadable at a glance as the byte-flag WIF format already is; a dedicated
  character costs one symbol to make the status actually visible without decoding.
* Root-node HRP name: <code>mn</code> ("master node") or <code>rn</code> ("root node")? <code>rn</code> is
  unregistered in SLIP-0173 as of this writing; <code>mn</code> needs the same check.
* Whether/how to specify folding origin info into a multisig descriptor's overall QR encoding at the Bitcoin Core
  level (an export-xpub feature), versus leaving that entirely to wallet software. Any such change is purely
  encoder-side: a standard QR reader decodes a multi-segment code identically to a single-segment one, so this
  imposes no requirement on decoders.

